  "notes": "The widest stylistic spread on 2000s–2010s radio — traditional weepers, arena party anthems and pop duets from one voice. The research documents \"two distinct grammars\" explicitly, which is why this model authors moods: \"traditional cuts use cross-stick and a small, dry kit; the party cuts use quarter kick, 8th/16th hats, claps layered on the backbeat, and a talk-box/chant breakdown section.\" Each mood carries its own harmony and melody so the two are not one mood with different drums — the traditional lane sits on I–IV–V with a narrow drawled line, the party lane on I–V–vi–IV and the minor i–VI–III–VII with a chant cell. The producer's stated priorities reach the session block directly: the key production questions are \"have we nailed the right tempo, key and feel?\", so tempo and key are authored as decisions with a real weighted key set rather than inherited defaults. Duet cuts \"alternate lead by 4- or 8-bar block with unison on the final chorus,\" which is the counter's answer weighting. ⚠ The programmed sub on the 2013+ party cuts is described as sitting *under* the electric bass, i.e. reinforcement, so no `bass808` block is authored and the model keeps the inherited live bass. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium) and the per-track key/tempo readings are [LOW-CONF]; the entry notes songdata's 152–206 figures fold onto the stated band. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the mood weights, the clap offsets, the counter ratio and the two mood BPM bands — the entry states three sub-bands (\"ballads 63–86, midtempo 90–120, uptempo 124–156\") and names no band for a *lane*, so 63–110 and 100–156 are this author’s blend of the adjacent ones rather than quoted figures; only the outer 63–156 is the research’s.",
